Recording Rules in Prometheus

🔹 What are Recording Rules? 🧠
 Recording rules are used to precompute PromQL query results and store them as new metrics.
Instead of running heavy queries repeatedly, Prometheus calculates them once and saves the result 📊
👉 Think of it as:
 “Save query result now, use it later quickly.”

🔹 Why are Recording Rules Needed? 🏗️
In large production systems, complex PromQL queries can become expensive ⚠️
Without recording rules:
 ❌ Queries become slow
 ❌ Dashboards load slowly
 ❌ High CPU usage on Prometheus server
👉 Recording rules solve this problem 🚀

🔹 How It Works: ⚡
✔ Prometheus runs query at intervals
 ✔ Result stored as a new metric
 ✔ Dashboards & alerts use stored metric

🔹 Example Recording Rule: 📊
record: job:http_requests:rate5m
expr: rate(http_requests_total[5m])

👉 This stores request rate as a reusable metric.

🔹 Performance Benefits: 🚀
✔ Faster dashboard loading 📈
 ✔ Reduced query execution time ⏱️
 ✔ Lower Prometheus server load 🖥️
 ✔ Better scalability for large environments 🌍

🔹 Real-world Example: 💡
Imagine a company monitoring 500+ servers 🏢
👉 A complex query calculating CPU usage for all systems runs every few seconds
Without recording rules:
Slow dashboards 🐢
High Prometheus load 🚨
With recording rules:
 ✔ Query result already stored
 ✔ Dashboards become fast ⚡
🔹 Key Concepts to Remember: 🧠
✔ Recording rules = Precomputed queries 📊
 ✔ Improve dashboard speed ⚡
 ✔ Reduce server load 🖥️
 ✔ Useful for large-scale monitoring 🌍
